        'slug' => 'is-it-sciatica-or-back-pain',
        'title' => 'Is It Sciatica or Just Back Pain? How to Tell the Difference',
        'topic' => 'Back & Neck', 'published_at' => '2026-08-12 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'spinal-decompression-therapy,chiropractic-care',
        'excerpt' => 'Back pain and sciatica often show up together, but they are not the same thing. Where the pain goes, and how it feels, says a lot about what is going on.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>People use the word sciatica for a lot of different kinds of back pain. Sometimes they are right. Often they are describing something else. The difference matters, because sciatica and ordinary back pain tend to have different causes and respond to different kinds of care.</p>
<h2>What sciatica actually is</h2>
<p>The sciatic nerve is the longest nerve in the body. It starts from several nerve roots in the low back, runs through the buttock and travels down the back of each leg. Sciatica is the name for pain that follows that path. It is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and it usually means a nerve root in the low back is being irritated or pressed on.</p>
<p>The most common cause is a herniated or bulging disc. Narrowing of the spaces where the nerves travel, called stenosis, is another, especially as we get older.</p>
<h2>Clues that point toward sciatica</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The leg hurts more than the back.</strong> This is one of the most useful signs. With sciatica, the leg pain is often the main complaint.</li>
<li><strong>The pain travels below the knee.</strong> Pain that runs down into the calf or foot is more likely to involve a nerve.</li>
<li><strong>It feels electric, burning or shooting.</strong> Nerve pain has a different quality from the dull ache of a strained muscle.</li>
<li><strong>There is tingling or numbness.</strong> Pins and needles in the leg or foot suggest a nerve is involved.</li>
<li><strong>Coughing, sneezing or sitting makes the leg worse.</strong> These raise pressure around the disc and nerve.</li>
<li><strong>It is usually on one side.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h2>Clues that point toward ordinary back pain</h2>
<p>Mechanical back pain from a strained muscle or an irritated joint tends to stay in the low back, or spread into the buttock or upper thigh without going much further. It often feels achy or stiff rather than sharp and electric, and it is typically linked to certain movements, like bending forward or standing up after sitting.</p>
<p>Pain can also travel into the buttock or thigh from the joints of the spine or the sacroiliac joint without a nerve being pinched. That is called referred pain, and it is one reason a proper exam is so helpful.</p>
<h2>Why the difference matters</h2>
<p>If your pain is coming from an irritated nerve, care tends to focus on taking pressure off that nerve and keeping an eye on how it is working. That might include spinal decompression, specific exercises and positions that ease the leg symptoms, and careful monitoring of strength and sensation.</p>
<p>If your pain is mechanical, care usually focuses on restoring movement and calming irritated joints and muscles, often with chiropractic care, soft tissue work and a gradual return to activity.</p>
<p>Plenty of people have a bit of both, which is another reason not to self diagnose from a list.</p>
<h2>What the evaluation looks at</h2>
<p>When you come in with back or leg pain, we will ask where the pain goes, what it feels like and what makes it better or worse. Then we test strength, reflexes and sensation in the legs, and check how the back and hips move. That usually tells us whether a nerve is involved and roughly where.</p>
<p>Imaging is not always needed straight away. It becomes more useful when symptoms are severe, when there are signs of nerve weakness, or when things are not improving as expected.</p>
<h2>When not to wait</h2>
<p>Get urgent medical care if leg pain comes with loss of bladder or bowel control, numbness around the groin or inner thighs, weakness that is getting worse, or symptoms in both legs at once. These are uncommon, but they need attention the same day.</p>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>If your leg hurts more than your back, the pain goes below the knee and it comes with tingling or numbness, there is a good chance a nerve is involved. Either way, the good news is that most people with sciatica or back pain improve without surgery. Getting the right diagnosis early simply makes it easier to choose care that fits.</p>
