        'slug' => 'desk-neck-pain-what-to-change-first',
        'title' => 'Your Desk Might Be Behind That Neck Pain. Here Is What to Change First',
        'topic' => 'Back & Neck', 'published_at' => '2026-08-20 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'chiropractic-care,class-iv-laser-therapy',
        'excerpt' => 'You do not need an expensive chair to ease a sore neck. A few small, boring changes to your setup and your day tend to make the biggest difference.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>If your neck aches by mid afternoon and your shoulders feel like they have crept up toward your ears, your workday is a likely suspect. Hours of looking at a screen, especially one that sits too low, put a steady load on the muscles and joints at the base of the neck.</p>
<p>The good news is that most of the fixes are simple. You do not need to buy a new desk or sit perfectly still in some ideal posture. In fact, sitting perfectly still is part of the problem.</p>
<h2>Start with the screen</h2>
<p>The most common issue we see is a screen that is too low. Laptops are the worst for this. When you look down at a screen for hours, your head drifts forward, and the muscles at the back of your neck have to work hard to hold it up.</p>
<p>Try this: sit back in your chair and look straight ahead. The top of your screen should be roughly at eye level, and the screen should be about an arm's length away. If you use a laptop all day, a cheap stand plus a separate keyboard and mouse can make a real difference.</p>
<h2>Bring your work closer</h2>
<p>If your keyboard or mouse is far away, you end up reaching forward, which rounds the shoulders and pulls the head along with them. Keep your elbows roughly by your sides and your keyboard close enough that your forearms can rest comfortably.</p>
<h2>Check your chair, but do not obsess</h2>
<p>Your feet should rest flat on the floor and your hips should be about level with or slightly higher than your knees. Sit back so the chair supports your lower back. A small cushion or rolled towel behind the low back helps some people.</p>
<p>Beyond that, chairs matter less than people think. The best posture is the next one.</p>
<h2>Move more often than feels necessary</h2>
<p>This is the change that helps most, and the one people skip. Your body does not like holding any position for a long time, even a good one. Aim to change position or get up at least every 30 to 45 minutes. Stand to take a phone call. Walk to refill your water. Look away from the screen and turn your head gently side to side.</p>
<p>If you tend to lose track of time, set a quiet reminder on your phone or computer. It feels silly for about a week, then it becomes habit.</p>
<h2>Two simple movements to try</h2>
<p><strong>Chin tucks.</strong> Sitting tall, gently draw your chin straight back, as if making a double chin, without tipping your head down. Hold for a few seconds and relax. Repeat a handful of times. This wakes up the deep neck muscles that help support your head.</p>
<p><strong>Shoulder blade squeezes.</strong> Let your arms hang, then gently draw your shoulder blades back and down. Hold briefly and release. This helps counter the rounded shoulder position that builds up during the day.</p>
<p>These should feel comfortable. If either one causes pain, tingling or dizziness, stop and talk to a provider.</p>
<h2>What about your phone?</h2>
<p>The same rules apply. Looking down at a phone for long stretches loads the neck the same way a low laptop does. Bring the phone up toward eye level when you can, and take breaks from scrolling.</p>
<h2>When to get it checked</h2>
<p>Desk related neck pain usually improves with these changes. It is worth getting evaluated if the pain has lasted more than a few weeks, if it keeps coming back, if you get frequent headaches, or if you notice pain, tingling or weakness spreading into your arm or hand. Those can be signs that a joint or nerve in the neck needs attention.</p>
<p>When we see people with this kind of neck pain, care often combines hands on treatment to restore movement with exercises and a look at the workstation and daily habits behind the problem. The treatment helps you feel better. The habits help it stay that way.</p>
