        'slug' => 'heel-pain-first-steps-in-the-morning',
        'title' => 'Why Your Heel Hurts With the First Steps of the Day',
        'topic' => 'Injury Care', 'published_at' => '2026-08-28 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'softwave-therapy,class-iv-laser-therapy',
        'excerpt' => 'Sharp heel pain when you get out of bed is a classic sign of plantar fasciitis. Here is what is going on and what tends to help.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>You swing your legs out of bed, put your foot down, and there it is. A sharp, stabbing pain under the heel that makes you hobble for the first few steps. After a few minutes of walking it eases off, only to come back after you have been sitting for a while.</p>
<p>That pattern is so typical of plantar fasciitis that many providers can make a good guess before they have even looked at your foot.</p>
<h2>What the plantar fascia does</h2>
<p>The plantar fascia is a thick band of tissue that runs along the bottom of your foot, from the heel to the toes. It supports the arch and acts a bit like a spring when you walk and run. When it gets overloaded, the area where it attaches to the heel bone can become irritated and painful.</p>
<p>Despite the name, ongoing plantar fasciitis is often less about inflammation and more about the tissue being worn down and struggling to keep up with the load placed on it.</p>
<h2>Why the first steps hurt most</h2>
<p>When you rest, especially overnight, the plantar fascia tightens up. Those first steps stretch it again quickly, and that sudden load on irritated tissue is what produces the sharp pain. As you keep walking, the tissue warms up and the pain often settles, which is why so many people describe it easing after a few minutes.</p>
<h2>Common reasons it starts</h2>
<ul>
<li>A sudden increase in walking, running or standing</li>
<li>Long hours on hard floors</li>
<li>Worn out or unsupportive shoes</li>
<li>Tight calf muscles or a stiff ankle</li>
<li>Weight gain, which increases load on the feet</li>
<li>Foot shape, such as very flat feet or high arches</li>
</ul>
<h2>What tends to help</h2>
<p><strong>Calf and foot stretches.</strong> Tight calves pull on the heel and increase strain on the plantar fascia. Gentle calf stretches, and stretching the bottom of the foot before you get out of bed, help many people.</p>
<p><strong>Supportive shoes.</strong> Wear shoes with good arch support and some cushioning, and try not to walk barefoot on hard floors while it is sore. Some people find that slipping on supportive sandals by the bed helps with those first steps.</p>
<p><strong>Adjusting your load for a while.</strong> You do not necessarily need to stop being active, but cutting back on the activity that flares it up and building back gradually gives the tissue a chance to settle.</p>
<p><strong>Strengthening.</strong> Over time, exercises that strengthen the foot and calf help the tissue handle load again. This is often the part that keeps the problem from coming back.</p>
<h2>When home care is not enough</h2>
<p>Plantar fasciitis can be stubborn. Some people improve within a few weeks, while others have pain that drags on for months. If you have been doing the basics and it is not getting better, it is worth having it looked at.</p>
<p>An evaluation can confirm that the pain really is coming from the plantar fascia rather than something else, such as a stress fracture or an irritated nerve. For persistent cases, treatments like SoftWave therapy, a non invasive acoustic wave treatment designed to stimulate a healing response in the tissue, or Class IV laser therapy may be options alongside stretching and strengthening. Whether they suit you depends on your evaluation.</p>
<h2>When to get seen sooner</h2>
<p>Get checked promptly if the heel pain started after a fall or injury, if you cannot put weight on the foot, if there is significant swelling or bruising, or if you have numbness or tingling in the foot. Those signs point away from simple plantar fasciitis.</p>
<p>For most people, though, morning heel pain is a sign that the foot has been asked to do a bit more than it was ready for. With some patience and the right adjustments, it usually settles.</p>
