        'slug' => 'iv-therapy-what-it-can-and-cannot-do',
        'title' => 'IV Therapy: An Honest Look at What It Can and Cannot Do',
        'topic' => 'Wellness', 'published_at' => '2026-09-05 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'iv-therapy,metabolic-optimization',
        'excerpt' => 'IV drips are everywhere now, often with big promises attached. Here is a plain look at what IV therapy is, who it may help and what it is not designed to do.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A few years ago, most people only saw an IV bag in a hospital. Now there are IV lounges in shopping centers and drip menus with names that promise energy, glowing skin or a cure for a rough weekend. It is no surprise people have questions.</p>
<p>We offer IV therapy, and we think it has a place. We also think you deserve a straight answer about what it can and cannot do.</p>
<h2>What IV therapy is</h2>
<p>IV therapy delivers fluids, and sometimes vitamins and minerals, directly into a vein through a small catheter. Because the fluid goes straight into the bloodstream, it does not have to pass through the digestive system first.</p>
<p>In a medical setting, the fluid is usually a saline based solution. A provider may add vitamins or minerals depending on your health and your reason for the visit.</p>
<h2>Where it may help</h2>
<p><strong>Hydration.</strong> This is the most straightforward use. If you are dehydrated after an illness, intense exercise or a long stretch in the heat, IV fluids rehydrate you quickly. For most people, though, drinking fluids works perfectly well, and that is usually the first suggestion.</p>
<p><strong>Recovery.</strong> Some people use IV therapy as part of their recovery after a demanding event or a bout of illness, once their provider confirms it is appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Specific deficiencies.</strong> For some people with certain nutrient deficiencies or absorption problems, getting nutrients directly into the bloodstream may be useful. That is a medical decision and usually involves testing.</p>
<h2>What it is not designed to do</h2>
<p>IV therapy is not a substitute for medical treatment of a disease. It does not detox your body, which already has a liver and kidneys for that job. It will not undo poor sleep, a demanding schedule or an unbalanced diet. And if you eat a reasonably varied diet and have no absorption problems, your body is generally good at getting what it needs from food.</p>
<p>We are wary of any place that sells IV therapy as a cure for everything. If a menu promises a specific result, it is worth asking what that promise is based on.</p>
<h2>Why screening matters</h2>
<p>An IV is a medical procedure, and it is not right for everyone. Before IV therapy, we review your health history and medications and check your vital signs. Some people, including those with certain heart, kidney or blood pressure conditions, may not be suitable candidates because extra fluid or certain ingredients could cause problems.</p>
<p>This is one of the biggest differences between IV therapy in a medical practice and a quick drip with no questions asked. The screening is there to keep you safe.</p>
<h2>What a visit is like</h2>
<p>After screening, a trained member of the clinical team places a small catheter in a vein, usually in the arm. You will feel a quick pinch. Then you sit back while the fluid runs, typically for about 30 to 60 minutes. Many people read or rest.</p>
<p>Afterwards, most people go back to their day. Mild bruising or soreness where the needle went in is common and usually fades within a few days. Tell the team straight away if you feel pain, burning, swelling, dizziness or shortness of breath during or after the infusion.</p>
<h2>Is it worth it?</h2>
<p>That depends on why you want it. For hydration and recovery in the right person, IV therapy can be a helpful option. As a regular replacement for sleep, water and good food, it is not. If you are curious, an evaluation is a good place to start. Your provider can tell you honestly whether it makes sense for you.</p>
