        'slug' => 'questions-to-ask-before-starting-a-glp1',
        'title' => 'Thinking About a GLP-1 for Weight Loss? Questions Worth Asking First',
        'topic' => 'Weight & Metabolism', 'published_at' => '2026-09-14 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'weight-loss,metabolic-optimization',
        'excerpt' => 'GLP-1 medications have changed the conversation about weight. Before you start one, these are the questions we think every patient should ask.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Few medications have had as much attention in recent years as the GLP-1 class, which includes semaglutide and tirzepatide. Friends talk about them, social media is full of them, and many people arrive at their first visit already asking for one by name.</p>
<p>These medications can be a useful tool for the right person. They are also prescription drugs with real side effects, and they work best as part of a wider plan. If you are considering one, here are the questions we encourage you to ask.</p>
<h2>1. Am I actually a candidate?</h2>
<p>Eligibility is decided by a licensed provider after an evaluation. That includes your weight history, other health conditions, current medications and sometimes lab work. Certain medical histories make these medications unsuitable, and some people are better served by a different approach. Wanting to lose weight on its own does not automatically make someone a candidate.</p>
<h2>2. What side effects should I expect?</h2>
<p>Digestive side effects are the most common, especially nausea, and they are often most noticeable when starting or increasing the dose. Constipation, diarrhea and reduced appetite are also common. There are less common but more serious risks too. Your provider should walk you through these before you begin and tell you which symptoms mean you should call straight away.</p>
<h2>3. How will it be monitored?</h2>
<p>Ask how often you will be seen, what will be checked and who to contact if you have problems. Regular follow up lets your provider adjust the dose, manage side effects and make sure the medication is still the right choice. A prescription with no follow up plan is a red flag.</p>
<h2>4. What else will be part of my plan?</h2>
<p>Medication can reduce appetite and help with weight, but it does not teach you how to eat well, protect your muscle or sleep better. The plans that tend to work best combine medication, when it is appropriate, with nutrition guidance, regular activity including some strength work, and support for the habits that matter in daily life.</p>
<p>Protecting muscle is worth a special mention. When people lose weight, some of that loss can come from muscle. Eating enough protein and doing some form of resistance exercise helps keep the weight you lose focused on fat.</p>
<h2>5. What happens if I stop?</h2>
<p>This is one of the most important questions and one that often goes unasked. For many people, appetite returns when the medication stops, and some or all of the weight can come back. That does not mean the medication failed. It means weight is influenced by long term biology. Talk with your provider about what the long term plan looks like before you start.</p>
<h2>6. Where is the medication coming from?</h2>
<p>Ask what exactly you are being prescribed and how it will be dispensed. You should know the name of the medication, the dose, how to use it and how to store it. If something about the source feels unclear, ask more questions before you go ahead.</p>
<h2>7. What are realistic expectations?</h2>
<p>Results vary a lot from person to person. We are cautious about anyone who promises a specific number of pounds by a specific date. A better question is how your provider will measure progress. Weight is one measure. Energy, blood sugar, blood pressure, waist size and how you feel day to day are others.</p>
<h2>A medication is a tool, not the whole plan</h2>
<p>For some people, a GLP-1 medication is a genuinely helpful part of medically supervised weight loss. For others, a different approach is a better fit. The way to find out is an honest conversation with a provider who looks at your whole health picture first and is happy to answer every one of these questions.</p>
