        'slug' => 'knee-pain-on-the-stairs',
        'title' => 'Knee Pain on the Stairs: Common Causes and What Usually Helps',
        'topic' => 'Joint Health', 'published_at' => '2026-09-22 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'knee-restoration,regenerative-joint-care',
        'excerpt' => 'Stairs ask a lot of your knees. If climbing up or coming down hurts, here are the usual suspects and the changes that tend to make a difference.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Stairs are often the first place people notice knee trouble. Walking on flat ground might feel fine, but climbing a flight to the office or carrying laundry down to the basement brings on an ache or a sharp twinge.</p>
<p>That is not a coincidence. Stairs put several times more load through the knee than walking on level ground, especially going down. So stairs tend to reveal knee problems early.</p>
<h2>Is it worse going up or coming down?</h2>
<p>It is worth noticing which one bothers you more, because it gives a useful clue.</p>
<p>Pain <strong>coming down</strong> stairs often points to the front of the knee, around or behind the kneecap. Going down asks the thigh muscles to control the bend of the knee while carrying your body weight, which loads the kneecap joint heavily.</p>
<p>Pain <strong>going up</strong> can also come from the kneecap area, but arthritis in the knee joint and weakness in the hip and thigh muscles often show up here too, because climbing requires you to push your weight up and forward.</p>
<h2>Common causes</h2>
<p><strong>Kneecap pain.</strong> Often called patellofemoral pain, this is a common cause of pain at the front of the knee, especially in active people and those who have recently increased their activity. It is usually linked to how the kneecap is loaded rather than damage to the joint.</p>
<p><strong>Osteoarthritis.</strong> Wear in the knee joint can cause pain, stiffness and sometimes swelling. Stairs, getting up from a low chair and kneeling are often the hardest tasks.</p>
<p><strong>Tendon pain.</strong> The tendon just below the kneecap can become irritated with jumping, running or a sudden increase in activity.</p>
<p><strong>Weak hips and thighs.</strong> When the muscles that control the hip and knee are not keeping up, the knee can drift inward on each step and take more strain than it should. Many people are surprised by how much the hip affects the knee.</p>
<h2>What usually helps</h2>
<p><strong>Strengthening.</strong> This is the foundation for most knee pain. Building strength in the thigh, hip and calf helps the knee handle load. Exercises are usually started gently and progressed, and it is normal to feel some mild discomfort that settles quickly.</p>
<p><strong>Adjusting how you use stairs for now.</strong> While you work on strength, it is fine to take stairs one at a time, use the handrail and lead with the stronger leg going up and the sore leg going down. These are temporary helpers, not permanent rules.</p>
<p><strong>Managing load.</strong> If a particular activity flares your knee, reduce it for a while rather than stopping everything. Staying active matters for joint health.</p>
<p><strong>Weight.</strong> For people carrying extra weight, even a modest loss reduces the load on the knee with every step.</p>
<p><strong>Treatment for irritated tissue.</strong> For some people, treatments such as SoftWave or Class IV laser therapy, or regenerative options for suitable joint problems, may be added alongside exercise. These are decided after an evaluation.</p>
<h2>When to get it looked at</h2>
<p>Get your knee checked if stair pain has lasted more than a few weeks, if the knee swells, locks, catches or gives way, or if it followed a twisting injury. Get seen urgently if the knee is hot, red and swollen, if you cannot put weight on it, or if you have calf pain and swelling.</p>
<p>An evaluation looks at the knee and the hip and ankle around it, so the plan targets the real cause. Most people with stair pain improve without surgery, and many get back to taking the stairs without thinking about it.</p>
