        'slug' => 'numb-or-tingling-feet-when-to-pay-attention',
        'title' => 'Numb or Tingling Feet: When to Pay Attention',
        'topic' => 'Nerve Health', 'published_at' => '2026-09-29 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'neuropathy-program,class-iv-laser-therapy',
        'excerpt' => 'A foot that falls asleep now and then is normal. Numbness, burning or tingling that keeps coming back is worth a closer look. Here is why.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Everyone has had a foot fall asleep after sitting cross legged for too long. That fizzy feeling fades within a minute and means nothing. But when numbness, tingling or burning in the feet starts to show up regularly, or never quite goes away, it deserves attention.</p>
<h2>What it might be</h2>
<p>One common cause is peripheral neuropathy, which means the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord are not working as they should. It often starts in the toes and feet, because those nerves are the longest in the body, and it may later reach the hands.</p>
<p>People describe neuropathy in all sorts of ways. Pins and needles. Burning, especially at night. Feet that feel cold even when they are warm, or as if you are walking on cotton wool. Sometimes it is not pain at all, just less feeling than there used to be.</p>
<p>Not all foot numbness is neuropathy, though. A pinched nerve in the low back can cause tingling in the foot. So can circulation problems and pressure on a nerve near the ankle. That is why it is worth having it checked rather than guessing.</p>
<h2>Common causes of neuropathy</h2>
<ul>
<li>Diabetes and high blood sugar, one of the most common causes</li>
<li>Low vitamin B12</li>
<li>Some medications</li>
<li>Regular heavy drinking</li>
<li>Thyroid and kidney conditions</li>
</ul>
<p>In some people, no clear cause is found. That can be frustrating, but it does not mean nothing can be done to help with comfort and function.</p>
<h2>Why loss of feeling matters</h2>
<p>It may sound strange, but feet that hurt less can be a bigger worry than feet that hurt more. When you cannot feel your feet properly, it is easy to miss a blister, a small cut or a shoe that rubs. For people with diabetes in particular, small foot injuries can become serious if they go unnoticed.</p>
<p>Reduced feeling also affects balance. Your feet constantly tell your brain about the ground beneath you. When that information is weaker, you may feel less steady, especially in the dark or on uneven ground.</p>
<h2>Simple things you can do now</h2>
<ul>
<li>Check your feet every day for cuts, blisters, redness or swelling. Use a mirror if needed.</li>
<li>Wear well fitting shoes and avoid going barefoot outdoors.</li>
<li>Test bath water with your elbow rather than your foot.</li>
<li>Keep the floor clear and use night lights to reduce the risk of falls.</li>
<li>If you have diabetes, keep working with your care team on blood sugar control.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What an evaluation looks at</h2>
<p>We start with where your symptoms began, how they have changed and what they feel like. Then we test sensation, reflexes, strength, circulation and balance, and check the low back. Lab work may be suggested to look for causes that can be treated, such as low B12.</p>
<p>Care then focuses on what matters to you, whether that is sleeping without burning feet, walking further or feeling steadier. A neuropathy program may include treatments for nerve symptoms, balance and strength training, foot care and coordination with your primary care provider. Nerves recover slowly and results vary, so we set realistic goals together.</p>
<h2>When to get seen quickly</h2>
<p>Seek urgent care if weakness or numbness spreads quickly over hours or days, if you have a foot wound or color change that is not healing, or if you have sudden numbness on one side of the body or face, which can be a sign of a stroke.</p>
<p>Otherwise, if your feet have been numb, tingling or burning for a few weeks or more, book an evaluation. Finding the cause is the first step to protecting your feet and your balance.</p>
