        'slug' => 'peptide-therapy-without-the-hype',
        'title' => 'Peptide Therapy Without the Hype',
        'topic' => 'Wellness', 'published_at' => '2026-10-03 09:00:00',
        'related' => 'peptide-therapy,hormone-optimization',
        'excerpt' => 'Peptides are having a moment online. Here is a calm explanation of what they are, why the details matter and how a responsible prescription decision is made.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you spend any time on health podcasts or social media, you have probably heard about peptides. They are promoted for recovery, fat loss, sleep, skin and aging, sometimes all at once. Some of that interest has a real basis. A lot of it is marketing.</p>
<p>Here is how we think about peptide therapy, and what we think you should know before considering it.</p>
<h2>What peptides are</h2>
<p>Peptides are short chains of amino acids, the same building blocks that make up proteins. Your body makes many peptides naturally, and a lot of them act as messengers, telling cells and tissues what to do.</p>
<p>Some medications are peptides. Insulin is one. The GLP-1 medications used for diabetes and weight loss are another. So the idea of a peptide medication is not new or exotic.</p>
<h2>Why "peptides" is not one thing</h2>
<p>This is the most important point. Talking about peptides as a group is a bit like talking about pills as a group. Different peptide therapies do different things, have different levels of research behind them, carry different risks and have different regulatory status. Some are well studied, approved medications. Others have much less evidence in people.</p>
<p>So when someone says peptides help with recovery or aging, the right response is to ask which one, for what, and based on what evidence.</p>
<h2>How a responsible decision is made</h2>
<p>We do not offer a peptide menu, and we would be cautious of anyone who does. A sensible process looks something like this:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start with the goal.</strong> What exactly do you want to improve, and how would you know if it was working?</li>
<li><strong>Review your health.</strong> Your history, current medications, possible reasons a therapy would be unsafe for you and, when useful, lab work.</li>
<li><strong>Decide whether any therapy fits.</strong> Sometimes the answer is a specific prescription. Often the answer is that sleep, nutrition, training or a different medical approach would do more.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up.</strong> If a therapy is prescribed, it should be reviewed regularly for response and side effects, and stopped if it is not helping.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Questions worth asking any provider</h2>
<ul>
<li>Why this therapy, for me, and what is the evidence?</li>
<li>What are the possible side effects and who should not use it?</li>
<li>How is it given, and will someone show me how?</li>
<li>Where does the medication come from?</li>
<li>How will we know if it is working, and when will we review it?</li>
<li>How long would I stay on it?</li>
</ul>
<p>A good provider will be glad you asked. If the answers are vague, take that as information.</p>
<h2>Keep the basics in view</h2>
<p>It is easy to get excited about a new therapy and overlook the things that quietly do most of the work. For recovery and healthy aging, sleep, strength training, protein intake, managing stress and staying active are hard to beat. A peptide prescription, when appropriate, sits on top of those foundations rather than replacing them.</p>
<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>Peptide therapy is not magic and it is not a scam. It is a group of very different treatments, some of which may be helpful for specific people with specific goals. The way to find out whether one belongs in your plan is a proper medical evaluation, not a menu.</p>
