        'excerpt' => 'A little preparation goes a long way. What to bring, what to think about beforehand and the questions that help you leave with a clear plan.',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>First appointments can feel rushed. You finally get in, talk for a while, and then on the drive home you remember the thing you meant to mention. Or you realise you are not quite sure what the plan is.</p>
<p>A bit of preparation helps a lot. Here is what we suggest to our own new patients.</p>
<h2>Think about your story before you come</h2>
<p>The conversation at the start of the visit is often the most useful part. It helps to have thought through a few things in advance:</p>
<ul>
<li>When did this start, and did anything set it off?</li>
<li>Has it been getting better, worse or staying the same?</li>
<li>What makes it better, and what makes it worse?</li>
<li>Are there times of day when it is worse?</li>
<li>What have you already tried, and did any of it help?</li>
</ul>
<p>You do not need perfect answers. Even rough notes on your phone make it easier to remember the details that matter.</p>
<h2>Know what you want to get back to</h2>
<p>This is the question people often have not considered, and it is one of the most helpful. Rather than only saying "my back hurts," tell us what it is stopping you from doing. Picking up your grandchild. Sleeping through the night. Running again. Getting through a workday without pain.</p>
<p>That goal shapes the plan and gives both of us a clear way to measure progress.</p>
<h2>Bring the useful paperwork</h2>
<ul>
<li>A list of your medications and supplements, with doses if you know them</li>
<li>Reports from any X-ray, MRI or other imaging related to the problem</li>
<li>Recent blood test results, especially for weight, energy or hormone concerns</li>
<li>Notes from other providers you have seen about the same issue</li>
<li>Your insurance card if you plan to use insurance</li>
</ul>
<p>If you do not have something, do not worry. We can usually request records or work with what is available.</p>
<h2>You do not need to know the answer</h2>
<p>Some people arrive having already decided which treatment they need, often because of something they read or a friend's experience. It is fine to mention it. Just keep an open mind. Working out what is actually going on is our job, and the right treatment follows from that.</p>
<h2>Write your questions down</h2>
<p>It is surprisingly easy to forget your questions once the appointment starts. Write them down and bring them. A few that are always worth asking:</p>
<ul>
<li>What do you think is causing this?</li>
<li>What are my options, including doing nothing for now?</li>
<li>What will the plan involve, and how long might it take?</li>
<li>How will we know if it is working?</li>
<li>What should I do, or avoid, at home in the meantime?</li>
<li>What signs mean I should call you sooner?</li>
</ul>
<h2>Wear something practical</h2>
<p>If your visit is about pain or movement, wear comfortable clothes that let you move and give access to the area, such as shorts for a knee problem. It makes the examination easier.</p>
<h2>Before you leave</h2>
<p>Make sure you understand three things: what the likely cause is, what happens next, and when you will be seen again or should check in. If anything is unclear, ask. A good visit should leave you with more clarity than you arrived with.</p>
